KG42.register("nodes-25cbbb71af6b137c68da06c5",{"id":"COMP-a774da994c2ca212","code":"PAR034","label":"Electricity-demand-side eligibility","definition":"Confirm that the entity participates in electricity trading as a consumer, a deficit energy buyer, or a flexible-demand player; organizational form, purchase-only authority, market activation, and conditions for continued participation are limited according to the source.","layer":"L1","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"participation.consumer","concept_ids":["participation.consumer","participation.prosumer"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Economic object: electricity used for consumption or to satisfy flexible demand","Economic role: consumer, deficit buyer, or flexible-demand player","Main permission: demand-side trading participation and source-limited authority"]},"exclusions":[["Resources include non-flexible load, time-coupled flexible load, and/or renewable energy; it is not required to possess all resources simultaneously.","Participation scope includes forward and real-time energy contract markets; two-way trading authority is not inferred from renewable energy configuration."],["An interconnected microgrid with a supply gap may trade with a microgrid that has surplus renewable generation.","The transaction is located within the distribution network, and this unit focuses on the deficit energy buyer."],["A local energy consumer may participate individually or as a local energy customer group.","Group participation does not automatically mean participation through a dedicated agent representing other users."],["Consumers may trade through Elecbay, with counterparties including suppliers, other consumers, and prosumers.","Applicable to low-voltage distribution networks; the consumer is not inferred to have electricity-sale authority from the counterparty category."],["The consumer must have flexible demand, participate independently in non-cooperative bidding, and not aggregate with other consumers or prosumers to obtain cooperative benefits.","N={1,2,…,i,…,n} is the set of all players, and n is the number of players; it does not contain only consumers."],["Prosumers with flexible demand are included in N and participate independently, without aggregating with consumers or other prosumers to obtain cooperative benefits.","Simulation uses a non-cooperative game for a single energy exchange period; N={1,2,…,i,…,n} denotes all players.","Case flexible demand is an electric water heater with a water tank, and scheduling keeps user demand satisfied; the case did not study energy storage systems or DG disconnection or derating scheduling, and these are not treated as general admission exclusions."],["The base model fixes the negotiating entity as either a producer or a consumer; when n∈Ω_c, it participates only as a consumer.","Under the injection sign convention, the bilateral decision satisfies P_nm≤0.","The two-bus case includes residential and industrial consumers; the fixed single-role assumption applies only to this base model."],["Ω_c is the consumer class in community Ω; when n∈Ω_c, p_nm≤0 holds for all m∈ω_n.","The source represents purchase-only permission by non-positive net transaction power, applicable to deterministic single-market time-unit P2P scheduling."],["Applicable to the Flexi User community, where the traded object is locally produced electric energy.","The system uses individually owned batteries, but it is not explicitly stated that battery ownership is a consumer admission condition."],["Community consumers obtain intra-community P2P participation eligibility only when P2P trading is enabled.","The P2P and STEP cases satisfy the enabling condition, and the participation scope is limited to within the community; this unit does not grant collective wholesale access rights."],["Applicable to the consumer role among prosumers; transactions must remain within the allocated DOE.","DOE supports local transactions under network constraints and does not add electricity-selling permission based on the prosumer label."],["The buyer consumes energy and purchases electricity from sellers or the grid, and may freely participate in bilateral transactions; the community is an example of a buyer, and participants connect to the local distribution network.","Continued participation requires \\rho^t_{sb}\\le\\mathrm{TLOU}; otherwise, exit; prices are indexed by seller, buyer, and time period.","TLOU is the grid price threshold; no numerical value is given."]],"members":[{"id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"Consumers with inflexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads and/or renewable sources may participate in the forward and real-time energy-contract markets. Scope: Consumer participation in the proposed forward and real-time bilateral contract network markets.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participation in the proposed forward and real-time bilateral contract network markets.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"3f2d148fb26468d6ce51a3ae8a86d53edd2a00280a720727ef47a5a0333f90a4","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Power-deficit microgrids may participate in energy trading with microgrids having excess renewable generation. Scope: Energy trading among interconnected microgrids in a distribution network. Condition: The participating microgrid is in deficit of power supplies.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy trading among interconnected microgrids in a distribution network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"fa8f6f4dac1b3ac44028e56e5a716d8d0b2faf6b16d82cc65e17095b2eb4e370","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Local energy consumers may participate as peers, individually or in groups of local energy customers. Scope: P2P energy trading among local energy customers.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"P2P energy trading among local energy customers.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-02"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"078a4c88d837c4e41730f87f2b6c7658d3e11d2372a60911e42d9afec66ab085","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R011","market_meaning":"Consumers may trade with energy suppliers and with other consumers and prosumers through Elecbay. Scope: Elecbay P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Elecbay P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-28"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011::v141-c-R011-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"1751e0202ac8dee697557b65e75a9e96d2ab90712df6d8d4f9bec00c5bc70aa9","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","anchor":true},{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","market_meaning":"Consumers with flexible demand are included as players in the modeled P2P bidding game. Scope: The modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The consumer has flexible demand. Consumer participants are not aggregated with other consumers or prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Competitive-participation assumption of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Competitive-participation assumption of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. | The modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-36"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C003"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"1c1215a806ea2e2609638c5d1d9a1d516d146bad9259813767a39c94562b729b","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","anchor":true},{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::V::participation.prosumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","market_meaning":"Prosumer participation is modeled as competitive, without aggregation with energy consumers and other prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. Prosumers with flexible demand are included as players in the bidding game. Scope: Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The prosumer has flexible demand.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. | Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-36"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C001","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C002","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C003"],"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","source_rule_sha256":"6f2f85ae034cc54721b54b0a3c64cd8d6b6296a1a16918674b1b33e754f2e169","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017","node_id":"participation.prosumer","function":"participation.prosumer","unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","anchor":true},{"id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"An agent assigned the consumer role is restricted to that operating role. Scope: Consumer participants in the two-bus MBED application. Condition: Under the base formulation assigning each negotiating agent a producer or consumer role.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participants in the two-bus MBED application.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g02-e26","v141-b-g02-e27"]},"constraint_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"0b69026610f62dcee82559892cb84e7eb17ffd753acf0d863eae92a62b2d24d2","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_ref":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"M01","anchor":true},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Consumers are forbidden to sell power; their bilateral traded-power quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer operating role in the deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch. Condition: The agent belongs to the consumer class Ω_c, with nonpositive net traded power.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer operating role in the deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e14"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"42131e46787ecd0038c27c34c42eb04ea0d3b691bdc73b2344d1dabd26949ddd","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Consumers within the community may participate in local trading of locally produced energy. Scope: Consumer participation within the Flexi User community with individually owned batteries.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participation within the Flexi User community with individually owned batteries.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g02-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"a5a91514db4538f931a28439d7ab94506bb2fa385549ef837d0795af5d2c5885","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Consumers are permitted to participate in P2P trade within the community. Scope: Local P2P trade within the community. Condition: P2P trade is enabled, as in the P2P and STEP market cases.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Local P2P trade within the community.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e38"]},"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"80074edc3aba46c49066f66566ebbf6c2095b194b7b109afa34d8a0fde6a231e","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"Consumer-role prosumers are permitted to trade in the LEM within their assigned DOEs. Scope: Consumer-side participation by prosumers in the proposed DOE-based LEM. Condition: Trading remains within the assigned DOEs.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer-side participation by prosumers in the proposed DOE-based LEM.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-E28"]},"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"27fa91eb91cb01a2eecc7c4d1115b041af0b4ad9fadfe4468374d90a119e56c8","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","anchor":true},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Continued consumer participation is conditional on the local P2P transaction price not exceeding TLOU. Scope: Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage. Condition: The P2P transaction price must not exceed TLOU; buyers quit otherwise. Energy buyers may trade freely with energy sellers and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. Scope: Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market. | Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"994b36819e26ccabbe86c561facc4cb1f0a665134ef08996156d526cc28f0433","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006","node_id":"participation.consumer","function":"participation.consumer","unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["03-bilateral_p2p","07-nash_bargaining","12-p2p_microgrid_trading","13-consensus_product_differentiation","17-exogenous_cost_allocation","26-battery_flexibility","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","43-operating_envelope_ev_market","49-residential_shared_storage_market"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract This paper proposes bilateral contract networks as a new scalable market design for peer-to","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Abstract—This paper proposes bilateral contract networks as a new scalable market design for peer-to-peer energy trading. ... This paper introduces real-time and forward markets, consisting of energy contracts offered between generators with fuel-based sources, suppliers acting as intermediaries and consumers with inflexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads and/or renewable sources. For each type of agent, utility-maximising preferences for real-time contracts and forward contracts are derived.","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Abstract—This paper proposes bilateral contract networks as a new scalable market design for peer-to-peer energy trading. ... This paper introduces real-time and forward markets, consisting of energy contracts offered between generators with fuel-based sources, suppliers acting as intermediaries and consumers with inflexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads and/or renewable sources. For each type of agent, utility-maximising preferences for real-time contracts and forward contracts are derived."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract In this paper, we study the interactions among di interconnected autonomous microgrids, and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, we study the interactions among interconnected autonomous microgrids, and develop a joint energy trading and scheduling strategy. Each interconnected microgrid not only schedules its local power supply and demand, but also trades energy with other microgrids in a distribution network. Specifically, microgrids with excessive renewable generations can trade with other microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefits. Since interconnected microgrids operate autonomously, they aim to optimize their own performance and expect to gain benefits through energy trading.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"In this paper, we study the interactions among interconnected autonomous microgrids, and develop a joint energy trading and scheduling strategy. Each interconnected microgrid not only schedules its local power supply and demand, but also trades energy with other microgrids in a distribution network. Specifically, microgrids with excessive renewable generations can trade with other microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefits. Since interconnected microgrids operate autonomously, they aim to optimize their own performance and expect to gain benefits through energy trading."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-02","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p01-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"1. Introduction","anchor_text":"system. go A peer in the P2P energy trading refers to one or a group of local ti energy customers, i","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A peer in the P2P energy trading refers to one or a group of local energy customers, including generators, consumers and prosumers. The peers buy or sell energy directly with each other without intermediation by conventional energy suppliers [3]. P2P energy trading is usually enabled by Information and Communication Technologies (ICT) -based online services [2].","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"A peer in the P2P energy trading refers to one or a group of local energy customers, including generators, consumers and prosumers. The peers buy or sell energy directly with each other without intermediation by conventional energy suppliers [3]. P2P energy trading is usually enabled by Information and Communication Technologies (ICT) -based online services [2]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-28","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M02-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3. Platform for Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"er energy suppliers, but also trade among each other. One peer is able to ts be either a seller or a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"“Elecbay” is one of the first platforms proposed for P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks, where energy consumers and prosumers are given the options to be able to not only trade energy with energy suppliers, but also trade among each other. One peer is able to be either a seller or a buyer at different time periods of a day, which is very different compared to the current GB electricity wholesale market, where generators are constantly sellers and suppliers are constantly buyers.","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R011","quote":"“Elecbay” is one of the first platforms proposed for P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks, where energy consumers and prosumers are given the options to be able to not only trade energy with energy suppliers, but also trade among each other. One peer is able to be either a seller or a buyer at different time periods of a day, which is very different compared to the current GB electricity wholesale market, where generators are constantly sellers and suppliers are constantly buyers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game.","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","quote":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-30","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4. Simulation of P2P bidding","anchor_text":"means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with is each other for achieving higher bene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","quote":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-30","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4. Simulation of P2P bidding","anchor_text":"means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with is each other for achieving higher bene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-34","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2. Simulation of a single time period using game theory","anchor_text":"th isting studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise sa tisfaction of end users will br","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"There are two basic types of games in game theory: cooperative games and non-cooperative games. A cooperative game is a game where groups of players enforce cooperative behaviour, and hence the game is a competition between coalitions of players, rather than between individual players. A non-cooperative game is a game in which players make decisions independently, for example, auctions and strategic voting [26]. A Nash equilibrium is a solution of a non-cooperative game involving two or more players, in which each player is assumed to know the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has anything to gain by changing only their own strategy [27].","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"There are two basic types of games in game theory: cooperative games and non-cooperative games. A cooperative game is a game where groups of players enforce cooperative behaviour, and hence the game is a competition between coalitions of players, rather than between individual players. A non-cooperative game is a game in which players make decisions independently, for example, auctions and strategic voting [26]. A Nash equilibrium is a solution of a non-cooperative game involving two or more players, in which each player is assumed to know the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has anything to gain by changing only their own strategy [27]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-35","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2. Simulation of a single time period using game theory","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"During the P2P bidding for a single energy exchange time period, the interaction among peers is modelled as a non-cooperative game, due to the assumptions made at the beginning of this section. The solution of the game, in which no peer is able to increase the benefit by modifying only its own placed orders, is the Nash equilibrium, which is considered as the result of P2P bidding through Elecbay.","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"During the P2P bidding for a single energy exchange time period, the interaction among peers is modelled as a non-cooperative game, due to the assumptions made at the beginning of this section. The solution of the game, in which no peer is able to increase the benefit by modifying only its own placed orders, is the Nash equilibrium, which is considered as the result of P2P bidding through Elecbay."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game.","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-37","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Strategies of a player are the decided status of flexible demand, which directly affect the amount of electricity to be injected to (positive value) or absorbed from (negative value) the Microgrid and are denoted by s_i^{j_i}, j_i = 1,2,… ,m_i, where m_i stands for the number of strategies that …","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"Strategies of a player are the decided status of flexible demand, which directly affect the amount of electricity to be injected to (positive value) or absorbed from (negative value) the Microgrid and are denoted by s_i^{j_i}, j_i = 1,2,… ,m_i, where m_i stands for the number of strategies that …"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-32","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1. Roles of flexible demand and storage in Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 220 (2018) 1–12 m and prosumers in P2P energy trading mainly rely on the scheduling o","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"DGs from intermittent renewable energy are able to be managed through disconnecting/reconnecting operations or derating maximum power outputs for energy trading purposes. However, those techniques are not investigated in this paper because it will be more beneficial for each individual peer to always keep its DG connected and generating maximum power, considering the zero marginal cost of renewable energy. Although energy storage systems are able to provide significant amount of flexibility for P2P energy trading, they are still generally very expensive. Therefore, this paper focuses on investigating the feasibility of P2P energy trading with flexible demand scheduling only. Specifically, electric water heaters with tanks are considered as representatives. The use of energy storage was not considered in this paper.","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"DGs from intermittent renewable energy are able to be managed through disconnecting/reconnecting operations or derating maximum power outputs for energy trading purposes. However, those techniques are not investigated in this paper because it will be more beneficial for each individual peer to always keep its DG connected and generating maximum power, considering the zero marginal cost of renewable energy. Although energy storage systems are able to provide significant amount of flexibility for P2P energy trading, they are still generally very expensive. Therefore, this paper focuses on investigating the feasibility of P2P energy trading with flexible demand scheduling only. Specifically, electric water heaters with tanks are considered as representatives. The use of energy storage was not considered in this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-33","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1. Roles of flexible demand and storage in Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"ng p p g g y gy g with ﬂexible demand scheduling only. Speciﬁcally, electric water hea- ters with ta","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Note that in this paper, the flexible demand is scheduled without sacrificing any satisfaction of end users. The flexible demands considered in the case study of this paper are electric water heaters with tanks, which have thermal storage capability. This capability results in the fact that there are many different heating schedules that are all able to totally satisfy users’ demand (in this case, to prepare enough hot water with required temperature for users throughout the day), and thus brings flexibility for scheduling. For example, to prepare required amount of hot water with required temperature to be used at 5:00 p.m., the water can be pre-heated either during 3:00–4:00 p.m. or during 4:00–5:00 p.m. without any difference in terms of satisfying users’ hot water need. In other words, by limiting the minimum and maximum water temperature in tank as presented in Section 4.2.1, the scheduling of water heaters will not cause any dissatisfaction for end users. This way of dealing with flexible demands is commonly used in many existing studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise satisfaction of end users will bring more flexibility for scheduling but involve more social cost at the same time, which is not considered in this paper.","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","quote":"Note that in this paper, the flexible demand is scheduled without sacrificing any satisfaction of end users. The flexible demands considered in the case study of this paper are electric water heaters with tanks, which have thermal storage capability. This capability results in the fact that there are many different heating schedules that are all able to totally satisfy users’ demand (in this case, to prepare enough hot water with required temperature for users throughout the day), and thus brings flexibility for scheduling. For example, to prepare required amount of hot water with required temperature to be used at 5:00 p.m., the water can be pre-heated either during 3:00–4:00 p.m. or during 4:00–5:00 p.m. without any difference in terms of satisfying users’ hot water need. In other words, by limiting the minimum and maximum water temperature in tank as presented in Section 4.2.1, the scheduling of water heaters will not cause any dissatisfaction for end users. This way of dealing with flexible demands is commonly used in many existing studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise satisfaction of end users will bring more flexibility for scheduling but involve more social cost at the same time, which is not considered in this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":"995","section":"II-A. Peer-to-Peer Trading","anchor_text":"m- The power set-points of an agent n are constrained by the ny power boundaries Pn and Pn, ms a Pn ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The role of each agent is restrained to either producer or consumer (P_n P̄_n ≥ 0) as it simplifies the following derivations. Hence, the decision variables are constrained in sign depending on whether the agent n is a producer (P_nm ≥ 0) or a consumer (P_nm ≤ 0).","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The role of each agent is restrained to either producer or consumer (P_n P̄_n ≥ 0) as it simplifies the following derivations. Hence, the decision variables are constrained in sign depending on whether the agent n is a producer (P_nm ≥ 0) or a consumer (P_nm ≤ 0)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e26","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":"999","section":"IV-A. Simulation Setup","anchor_text":"is e- A. Simulation Setup ly p- The setup is composed of 12 agents: 6 producers includ- ta ing two w","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"IV. APPLICATION AND CASE STUDIES\n... A. Simulation Setup\n    The setup is composed of 12 agents: 6 producers includ-ing two wind turbines, 2 solar PV and 2 fossil generators and 6 consumers including 4 household consumers and 2 industrial consumers. The sequences of wind and solar PV production are taken from [20] and [21]. The consumption sequences as well as the ﬂexibility capacity are derived from [21]. The non time-varying market participants are taken from [5]. Note that compared to [5], non-zero lower boundary con-straints have been added for the generators. The capacities of the generators and consumers are adapted to be comparable. It was chosen to model the PV and the wind turbines as must-take generators (Pn = Pn). This allow do deﬁne for them a virtual cost function (an and bn) without affecting the optimal solution.","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"IV. APPLICATION AND CASE STUDIES\n... A. Simulation Setup\n    The setup is composed of 12 agents: 6 producers includ-ing two wind turbines, 2 solar PV and 2 fossil generators and 6 consumers including 4 household consumers and 2 industrial consumers. The sequences of wind and solar PV production are taken from [20] and [21]. The consumption sequences as well as the ﬂexibility capacity are derived from [21]. The non time-varying market participants are taken from [5]. Note that compared to [5], non-zero lower boundary con-straints have been added for the generators. The capacities of the generators and consumers are adapted to be comparable. It was chosen to model the PV and the wind turbines as must-take generators (Pn = Pn). This allow do deﬁne for them a virtual cost function (an and bn) without affecting the optimal solution."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":"1000","section":"IV-A. Simulation Setup","anchor_text":"buses. cost function (an and bn) without affecting the optimal solution. Another option would be to ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The trades are differentiated through a single criterion (cnm =\ncnγnm) that aims at increasing self and local consumption. For\nthat purpose γnm  is chosen to be the euclidean distance be-\ntween agents n and m. The agents are split into two buses (see\nFig. 3), with 3 generators and 3 loads each. Note that the MBED\nmodel can be operated without a corresponding network layer.","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The trades are differentiated through a single criterion (cnm =\ncnγnm) that aims at increasing self and local consumption. For\nthat purpose γnm  is chosen to be the euclidean distance be-\ntween agents n and m. The agents are split into two buses (see\nFig. 3), with 3 generators and 3 loads each. Note that the MBED\nmodel can be operated without a corresponding network layer."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"997","section":"II-C. MBED Formulation","anchor_text":"SORIN et al.: CONSENSUS-BASED APPROACH TO PEER-TO-PEER ELECTRICITY MARK Pnm + Pmn = 0 ∀(n, m) ∈(Ω, ω","equation_id":"(9c), (9d), (9e)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"P_nm + P_mn = 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω, ω_n) (9c) P_nm ≥ 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω_p, ω_n) (9d) P_nm ≤ 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω_c, ω_n) (9e) where D = (p_n ∈ R^{|ω_n|})_{n∈Ω}. Ω_p and Ω_c are the sets of producers and consumers, respectively.","paper":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"P_nm + P_mn = 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω, ω_n) (9c) P_nm ≥ 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω_p, ω_n) (9d) P_nm ≤ 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω_c, ω_n) (9e) where D = (p_n ∈ R^{|ω_n|})_{n∈Ω}. Ω_p and Ω_c are the sets of producers and consumers, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"for its use of the power system. In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the of only","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"y ent pn = pnm n ∈Ω (1c) e.g. m∈ωn on- gh pn ⩽pn ⩽pn n ∈Ω (1d) ap- pnm ⩾0 n ∈Ωg (1e) ork cal pnm ⩽0 ","equation_id":"Eq. (1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,\\theta_i\\in\\mathcal{N}}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}f_n(p_n) (1a); s.t. P=-P^{T} (1b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (1c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (1d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (1e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (1f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p (1g); q_{ij}=Y_{ij}(\\theta_j-\\theta_i)\\le C_{ij}, (i,j)\\in\\mathcal{L} (1h); \\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal{N}_i}p_n=\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{L}}q_{ij}, i\\in\\mathcal{N} (1i).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"\\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,\\theta_i\\in\\mathcal{N}}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}f_n(p_n) (1a); s.t. P=-P^{T} (1b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (1c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (1d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (1e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (1f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p (1g); q_{ij}=Y_{ij}(\\theta_j-\\theta_i)\\le C_{ij}, (i,j)\\in\\mathcal{L} (1h); \\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal{N}_i}p_n=\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{L}}q_{ij}, i\\in\\mathcal{N} (1i)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"RICITY MARKETS 2555 as violated. It can be noted that in this case h depends on the real s, power in","equation_id":"Eq. (3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Network constraints (1h)–(1i) can be condensed in a regularization function h, equal to 0 if they are respected and +∞ if they are violated. It can be noted that in this case h depends on the real power injections p_{n\\in\\Omega}. Then, problem (1) can be written as \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}f_n(p_n)+h(p_{n\\in\\Omega}) (3a); s.t. P=-P^{T} (3b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (3c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (3d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (3e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (3f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (3g).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"Network constraints (1h)–(1i) can be condensed in a regularization function h, equal to 0 if they are respected and +∞ if they are violated. It can be noted that in this case h depends on the real power injections p_{n\\in\\Omega}. Then, problem (1) can be written as \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}f_n(p_n)+h(p_{n\\in\\Omega}) (3a); s.t. P=-P^{T} (3b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (3c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (3d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (3e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (3f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (3g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Market design for peer-to-peer trading","anchor_text":"he ability to sell excess electricity from renewable energy sources to peers. Each of the two setups","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Market design for peer-to-peer trading","anchor_text":"he ability to sell excess electricity from renewable energy sources to peers. Each of the two setups","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"market. In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among ec- prosumers and consumers in a com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among prosumers and consumers in a community. Peers can trade locally produced and stored energy at a dynamic, half-hourly varying P2P price. Some prosumers and consumers have own batteries that can be charged with local generation and from the grid (see, e.g., [26]).","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among prosumers and consumers in a community. Peers can trade locally produced and stored energy at a dynamic, half-hourly varying P2P price. Some prosumers and consumers have own batteries that can be charged with local generation and from the grid (see, e.g., [26])."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e38","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p09-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"As of today, electricity end-users are at the end of the supply era chain in a still predominant top","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Today’s UK market under dynamic pricing – assuming smart metering – serves in this paper as our base case. To identify the value of each of the proposed local market features – P2P trade and battery storage –, three further cases are compared to the base case: We add P2P trade and battery storage possibilities one by one to investigate their sole values to the system. In short, we apply our model to four cases that distinguish by their options of battery storage and P2P trade. The possibility of feeding from the community into the grid is enabled in all cases. We define the following: • The Base case is defined as today’s electricity market under dynamic real-time pricing. It is not possible to directly trade with other peers, and no electricity storage possibilities are deployed within this case. • The BAT market introduces the possibility of battery storage. Direct trade between the peers is disabled. • The P2P market includes the direct P2P trade connection in the community. The possibility of storage is, however, not implemented. • The STEP market offers both battery storage and P2P trade options within the community.","paper":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Today’s UK market under dynamic pricing – assuming smart metering – serves in this paper as our base case. To identify the value of each of the proposed local market features – P2P trade and battery storage –, three further cases are compared to the base case: We add P2P trade and battery storage possibilities one by one to investigate their sole values to the system. In short, we apply our model to four cases that distinguish by their options of battery storage and P2P trade. The possibility of feeding from the community into the grid is enabled in all cases. We define the following: • The Base case is defined as today’s electricity market under dynamic real-time pricing. It is not possible to directly trade with other peers, and no electricity storage possibilities are deployed within this case. • The BAT market introduces the possibility of battery storage. Direct trade between the peers is disabled. • The P2P market includes the direct P2P trade connection in the community. The possibility of storage is, however, not implemented. • The STEP market offers both battery storage and P2P trade options within the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"Contributions and Organization","anchor_text":"p nd networks to secure network operations by managing rb prosumers’ energy imports/exports. by • A ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed DOE-based LEM framework enables secure network operation for consumers trading in the LEM using assigned DOEs. The presented DOE calculation algorithm is computationally efficient and can be implemented for near real-time market clearing.","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The proposed DOE-based LEM framework enables secure network operation for consumers trading in the LEM using assigned DOEs. The presented DOE calculation algorithm is computationally efficient and can be implemented for near real-time market clearing."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p07-c2r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"Prosumers Bidding Strategy","anchor_text":"p y , j,t j,t j,t j,t y g g quantities of prosumer j with the peers and the upstream grid Vs- in eac","equation_id":"(29)-(30)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Prosumers are allowed to trade in the LEM within the assigned DOEs P̂_{j,t} confirming the network-constrained operation. The sum of the buying energy of prosumer j at time t with the peers and the upstream grid should be equal to or less than the DOE limits P̂_{j,t}(> 0), and the sum of the selling energy with the peers and the upstream grid should be equal to or greater than the DOE limits P̂_{j,t}(≤ 0) as in (29) and (30), respectively. P^{pb}_{j,t} + P^{gb}_{j,t} ≤ P̂_{j,t}, if P̂_{j,t} > 0; ∀j∈J,∀t∈T, (29) P^{ps}_{j,t} + P^{gs}_{j,t} ≥ P̂_{j,t}, if P̂_{j,t} ≤ 0; ∀j∈J,∀t∈T. (30)","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"Prosumers are allowed to trade in the LEM within the assigned DOEs P̂_{j,t} confirming the network-constrained operation. The sum of the buying energy of prosumer j at time t with the peers and the upstream grid should be equal to or less than the DOE limits P̂_{j,t}(> 0), and the sum of the selling energy with the peers and the upstream grid should be equal to or greater than the DOE limits P̂_{j,t}(≤ 0) as in (29) and (30), respectively. P^{pb}_{j,t} + P^{gb}_{j,t} ≤ P̂_{j,t}, if P̂_{j,t} > 0; ∀j∈J,∀t∈T, (29) P^{ps}_{j,t} + P^{gs}_{j,t} ≥ P̂_{j,t}, if P̂_{j,t} ≤ 0; ∀j∈J,∀t∈T. (30)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2 System configuration","anchor_text":"b­ nt The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 in­ troduces a representative sy","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The most representative structure of the peer-to-peer energy trading market with shared energy storage units is shown in Fig. 1. In such a P2P market, a participant who has excessive energy and sells energy to other participants or the power grid is defined as a typical energy seller, e.g., a rooftop PV plant. A participant who consumes energy and buys energy from energy sellers or power grid is identified as a typical energy buyer, e.g., a community. Energy sellers and buyers in the P2P market can trade with each other freely and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. The solid arrows present the P2P transaction between typical energy sellers and energy buyers. All participants are connected with the local distribution network and the energy trading can be finished through physical power transmission lines.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The most representative structure of the peer-to-peer energy trading market with shared energy storage units is shown in Fig. 1. In such a P2P market, a participant who has excessive energy and sells energy to other participants or the power grid is defined as a typical energy seller, e.g., a rooftop PV plant. A participant who consumes energy and buys energy from energy sellers or power grid is identified as a typical energy buyer, e.g., a community. Energy sellers and buyers in the P2P market can trade with each other freely and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. The solid arrows present the P2P transaction between typical energy sellers and energy buyers. All participants are connected with the local distribution network and the energy trading can be finished through physical power transmission lines."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["buyer_role_and_price_conditioned_participation","community_consumer_eligibility","conditional_community_peer_participation","consumer_participation_eligibility","consumer_role_eligibility","deficit_buyer_participation","fixed_consumer_role","flexible_demand_player_eligibility","operating_envelope_participation_eligibility","purchase_only_participant_role"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"KG40-L1-SC-L1-2a489524c6392357","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L1","ontological_kind":"SC-L1-2a489524c6392357"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Confirm that the entity participates in electricity trading as a consumer, a deficit energy buyer, or a flexible-demand player; organizational form, purchase-only authority, market activation, and conditions for continued participation are limited according to the source.","identity_fields":["Economic object: electricity used for consumption or to satisfy flexible demand","Economic role: consumer, deficit buyer, or flexible-demand player","Main permission: demand-side trading participation and source-limited authority"]},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"03-bilateral_p2p","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"Consumers with inflexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads and/or renewable sources may participate in the forward and real-time energy-contract markets. Scope: Consumer participation in the proposed forward and real-time bilateral contract network markets.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participation in the proposed forward and real-time bilateral contract network markets.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"3f2d148fb26468d6ce51a3ae8a86d53edd2a00280a720727ef47a5a0333f90a4"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers with inflexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads and/or renewable sources may participate in the forward and real-time energy-contract markets. Scope: Consumer participation in the proposed forward and real-time bilateral contract network markets.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract This paper proposes bilateral contract networks as a new scalable market design for peer-to","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Abstract—This paper proposes bilateral contract networks as a new scalable market design for peer-to-peer energy trading. ... This paper introduces real-time and forward markets, consisting of energy contracts offered between generators with fuel-based sources, suppliers acting as intermediaries and consumers with inflexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads and/or renewable sources. For each type of agent, utility-maximising preferences for real-time contracts and forward contracts are derived."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"07-nash_bargaining","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Power-deficit microgrids may participate in energy trading with microgrids having excess renewable generation. Scope: Energy trading among interconnected microgrids in a distribution network. Condition: The participating microgrid is in deficit of power supplies.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy trading among interconnected microgrids in a distribution network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"fa8f6f4dac1b3ac44028e56e5a716d8d0b2faf6b16d82cc65e17095b2eb4e370"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Power-deficit microgrids may participate in energy trading with microgrids having excess renewable generation. Scope: Energy trading among interconnected microgrids in a distribution network. Condition: The participating microgrid is in deficit of power supplies.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract In this paper, we study the interactions among di interconnected autonomous microgrids, and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, we study the interactions among interconnected autonomous microgrids, and develop a joint energy trading and scheduling strategy. Each interconnected microgrid not only schedules its local power supply and demand, but also trades energy with other microgrids in a distribution network. Specifically, microgrids with excessive renewable generations can trade with other microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefits. Since interconnected microgrids operate autonomously, they aim to optimize their own performance and expect to gain benefits through energy trading."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Local energy consumers may participate as peers, individually or in groups of local energy customers. Scope: P2P energy trading among local energy customers.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"P2P energy trading among local energy customers.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-02"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"078a4c88d837c4e41730f87f2b6c7658d3e11d2372a60911e42d9afec66ab085"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local energy consumers may participate as peers, individually or in groups of local energy customers. Scope: P2P energy trading among local energy customers.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-02"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-02","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p01-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"1. Introduction","anchor_text":"system. go A peer in the P2P energy trading refers to one or a group of local ti energy customers, i","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A peer in the P2P energy trading refers to one or a group of local energy customers, including generators, consumers and prosumers. The peers buy or sell energy directly with each other without intermediation by conventional energy suppliers [3]. P2P energy trading is usually enabled by Information and Communication Technologies (ICT) -based online services [2]."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::participation.consumer","paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R011","market_meaning":"Consumers may trade with energy suppliers and with other consumers and prosumers through Elecbay. Scope: Elecbay P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Elecbay P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-28"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011::v141-c-R011-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"1751e0202ac8dee697557b65e75a9e96d2ab90712df6d8d4f9bec00c5bc70aa9"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R011-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers may trade with energy suppliers and with other consumers and prosumers through Elecbay. Scope: Elecbay P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-28"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011::v141-c-R011-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R011","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-28","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M02-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3. Platform for Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"er energy suppliers, but also trade among each other. One peer is able to ts be either a seller or a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"“Elecbay” is one of the first platforms proposed for P2P energy trading in LV distribution networks, where energy consumers and prosumers are given the options to be able to not only trade energy with energy suppliers, but also trade among each other. One peer is able to be either a seller or a buyer at different time periods of a day, which is very different compared to the current GB electricity wholesale market, where generators are constantly sellers and suppliers are constantly buyers."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.consumer","paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","market_meaning":"Consumers with flexible demand are included as players in the modeled P2P bidding game. Scope: The modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The consumer has flexible demand. Consumer participants are not aggregated with other consumers or prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Competitive-participation assumption of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Competitive-participation assumption of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. | The modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-36"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C003"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"1c1215a806ea2e2609638c5d1d9a1d516d146bad9259813767a39c94562b729b"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R016-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers with flexible demand are included as players in the modeled P2P bidding game. Scope: The modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The consumer has flexible demand.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R016-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumer participants are not aggregated with other consumers or prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Competitive-participation assumption of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-30","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4. Simulation of P2P bidding","anchor_text":"means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with is each other for achieving higher bene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R016-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The player set of the modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game is denoted N = {1, 2, …, i, …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game and N is the set of players. The source states that the players are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Scope: The modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The player has flexible demand. This rule's frozen graph declares only participation.consumer, so this constraint records the player-set formula as it bears on consumer participation; the prosumer subset is not asserted by this rule.","expression":"N = {1, 2, \\ldots, i, \\ldots, n}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R016-TH001","v141-c-R016-TH002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R016-TH001","symbol":"n","meaning":"number of players in the modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::theta::v141-c-R016-TH001"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R016-TH002","symbol":"N","meaning":"set of players in the modeled non-cooperative P2P bidding game","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"N = {1, 2, …, i, …, n}"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::theta::v141-c-R016-TH002"}]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer","paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","function":"participation.prosumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::V::participation.prosumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R017","market_meaning":"Prosumer participation is modeled as competitive, without aggregation with energy consumers and other prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. Prosumers with flexible demand are included as players in the bidding game. Scope: Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The prosumer has flexible demand.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. | Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-36"]},"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C001","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C002","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C003"],"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","source_rule_sha256":"6f2f85ae034cc54721b54b0a3c64cd8d6b6296a1a16918674b1b33e754f2e169"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R017-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Prosumer participation is modeled as competitive, without aggregation with energy consumers and other prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-34","v141-b-g01-ev-35"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R017","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-30","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4. Simulation of P2P bidding","anchor_text":"means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with is each other for achieving higher bene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-34","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2. Simulation of a single time period using game theory","anchor_text":"th isting studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise sa tisfaction of end users will br","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"There are two basic types of games in game theory: cooperative games and non-cooperative games. A cooperative game is a game where groups of players enforce cooperative behaviour, and hence the game is a competition between coalitions of players, rather than between individual players. A non-cooperative game is a game in which players make decisions independently, for example, auctions and strategic voting [26]. A Nash equilibrium is a solution of a non-cooperative game involving two or more players, in which each player is assumed to know the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has anything to gain by changing only their own strategy [27]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-35","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2. Simulation of a single time period using game theory","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"During the P2P bidding for a single energy exchange time period, the interaction among peers is modelled as a non-cooperative game, due to the assumptions made at the beginning of this section. The solution of the game, in which no peer is able to increase the benefit by modifying only its own placed orders, is the Nash equilibrium, which is considered as the result of P2P bidding through Elecbay."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R017-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Prosumers with flexible demand are included as players in the bidding game. Scope: Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The prosumer has flexible demand.","expression":"\\forall i \\in \\{\\text{prosumers with flexible demand}\\}: i \\in N","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36","v141-b-g01-ev-37","v141-b-g01-ev-32","v141-b-g01-ev-33"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R017","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-37","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Strategies of a player are the decided status of flexible demand, which directly affect the amount of electricity to be injected to (positive value) or absorbed from (negative value) the Microgrid and are denoted by s_i^{j_i}, j_i = 1,2,… ,m_i, where m_i stands for the number of strategies that …"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-32","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1. Roles of flexible demand and storage in Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 220 (2018) 1–12 m and prosumers in P2P energy trading mainly rely on the scheduling o","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"DGs from intermittent renewable energy are able to be managed through disconnecting/reconnecting operations or derating maximum power outputs for energy trading purposes. However, those techniques are not investigated in this paper because it will be more beneficial for each individual peer to always keep its DG connected and generating maximum power, considering the zero marginal cost of renewable energy. Although energy storage systems are able to provide significant amount of flexibility for P2P energy trading, they are still generally very expensive. Therefore, this paper focuses on investigating the feasibility of P2P energy trading with flexible demand scheduling only. Specifically, electric water heaters with tanks are considered as representatives. The use of energy storage was not considered in this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-33","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1. Roles of flexible demand and storage in Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"ng p p g g y gy g with ﬂexible demand scheduling only. Speciﬁcally, electric water hea- ters with ta","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Note that in this paper, the flexible demand is scheduled without sacrificing any satisfaction of end users. The flexible demands considered in the case study of this paper are electric water heaters with tanks, which have thermal storage capability. This capability results in the fact that there are many different heating schedules that are all able to totally satisfy users’ demand (in this case, to prepare enough hot water with required temperature for users throughout the day), and thus brings flexibility for scheduling. For example, to prepare required amount of hot water with required temperature to be used at 5:00 p.m., the water can be pre-heated either during 3:00–4:00 p.m. or during 4:00–5:00 p.m. without any difference in terms of satisfying users’ hot water need. In other words, by limiting the minimum and maximum water temperature in tank as presented in Section 4.2.1, the scheduling of water heaters will not cause any dissatisfaction for end users. This way of dealing with flexible demands is commonly used in many existing studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise satisfaction of end users will bring more flexibility for scheduling but involve more social cost at the same time, which is not considered in this paper."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R017-C003","kind":"logical","role":"supporting","statement":"The player set of the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game is N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game and N is the set of players in the game. Scope: The single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","expression":"N = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, i, \\ldots, n\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36"],"ref":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R017","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","mechanism_id":"M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"An agent assigned the consumer role is restricted to that operating role. Scope: Consumer participants in the two-bus MBED application. Condition: Under the base formulation assigning each negotiating agent a producer or consumer role.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participants in the two-bus MBED application.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g02-e26","v141-b-g02-e27"]},"constraint_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"0b69026610f62dcee82559892cb84e7eb17ffd753acf0d863eae92a62b2d24d2"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"An agent assigned the consumer role is restricted to that operating role. Scope: Consumer participants in the two-bus MBED application. Condition: Under the base formulation assigning each negotiating agent a producer or consumer role.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g02-e26","v141-b-g02-e27"],"ref":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":"995","section":"II-A. Peer-to-Peer Trading","anchor_text":"m- The power set-points of an agent n are constrained by the ny power boundaries Pn and Pn, ms a Pn ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The role of each agent is restrained to either producer or consumer (P_n P̄_n ≥ 0) as it simplifies the following derivations. Hence, the decision variables are constrained in sign depending on whether the agent n is a producer (P_nm ≥ 0) or a consumer (P_nm ≤ 0)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e26","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":"999","section":"IV-A. Simulation Setup","anchor_text":"is e- A. Simulation Setup ly p- The setup is composed of 12 agents: 6 producers includ- ta ing two w","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"IV. APPLICATION AND CASE STUDIES\n... A. Simulation Setup\n    The setup is composed of 12 agents: 6 producers includ-ing two wind turbines, 2 solar PV and 2 fossil generators and 6 consumers including 4 household consumers and 2 industrial consumers. The sequences of wind and solar PV production are taken from [20] and [21]. The consumption sequences as well as the ﬂexibility capacity are derived from [21]. The non time-varying market participants are taken from [5]. Note that compared to [5], non-zero lower boundary con-straints have been added for the generators. The capacities of the generators and consumers are adapted to be comparable. It was chosen to model the PV and the wind turbines as must-take generators (Pn = Pn). This allow do deﬁne for them a virtual cost function (an and bn) without affecting the optimal solution."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":"1000","section":"IV-A. Simulation Setup","anchor_text":"buses. cost function (an and bn) without affecting the optimal solution. Another option would be to ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The trades are differentiated through a single criterion (cnm =\ncnγnm) that aims at increasing self and local consumption. For\nthat purpose γnm  is chosen to be the euclidean distance be-\ntween agents n and m. The agents are split into two buses (see\nFig. 3), with 3 generators and 3 loads each. Note that the MBED\nmodel can be operated without a corresponding network layer."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Under the base formulation assigning each negotiating agent a producer or consumer role, a consumer agent's bilateral decision variables are sign-constrained to be nonpositive: P_nm \u003c= 0 for every n in the consumer set Omega_c and every neighboring agent m in omega_n.","expression":"P_{nm} \\le 0 \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in (\\Omega_c, \\omega_n)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-X001"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e17"],"ref":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":"995","section":"II-A. Peer-to-Peer Trading","anchor_text":"m- The power set-points of an agent n are constrained by the ny power boundaries Pn and Pn, ms a Pn ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The role of each agent is restrained to either producer or consumer (P_n P̄_n ≥ 0) as it simplifies the following derivations. Hence, the decision variables are constrained in sign depending on whether the agent n is a producer (P_nm ≥ 0) or a consumer (P_nm ≤ 0)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-8ce0608b1a-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"997","section":"II-C. MBED Formulation","anchor_text":"SORIN et al.: CONSENSUS-BASED APPROACH TO PEER-TO-PEER ELECTRICITY MARK Pnm + Pmn = 0 ∀(n, m) ∈(Ω, ω","equation_id":"(9c), (9d), (9e)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"P_nm + P_mn = 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω, ω_n) (9c) P_nm ≥ 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω_p, ω_n) (9d) P_nm ≤ 0 ∀(n, m) ∈ (Ω_c, ω_n) (9e) where D = (p_n ∈ R^{|ω_n|})_{n∈Ω}. Ω_p and Ω_c are the sets of producers and consumers, respectively."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-X001","symbol":"P_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral traded quantity (net injection) of negotiating agent n with neighboring agent m under the MBED injection convention; for an agent assigned the consumer role, this quantity is constrained to be nonpositive.","kind":"operational","domain":"real (source gives D=(p_n in R^{|omega_n|})_{n in Omega} for the agent decision vector)","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-X001"}],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Consumers are forbidden to sell power; their bilateral traded-power quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer operating role in the deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch. Condition: The agent belongs to the consumer class Ω_c, with nonpositive net traded power.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer operating role in the deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e14"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"42131e46787ecd0038c27c34c42eb04ea0d3b691bdc73b2344d1dabd26949ddd"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers are forbidden to sell power; their bilateral traded-power quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer operating role in the deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch. Condition: The agent belongs to the consumer class Ω_c, with nonpositive net traded power.","expression":"p_{nm} \\le 0, \\quad \\forall n \\in \\Omega_c,\\ m \\in \\omega_n","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-x-pnm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-theta-omega-c","v141-c-R006-theta-omega-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e12","v141-b-g01-e17"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"for its use of the power system. In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the of only","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"y ent pn = pnm n ∈Ω (1c) e.g. m∈ωn on- gh pn ⩽pn ⩽pn n ∈Ω (1d) ap- pnm ⩾0 n ∈Ωg (1e) ork cal pnm ⩽0 ","equation_id":"Eq. (1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,\\theta_i\\in\\mathcal{N}}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}f_n(p_n) (1a); s.t. P=-P^{T} (1b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (1c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (1d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (1e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (1f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p (1g); q_{ij}=Y_{ij}(\\theta_j-\\theta_i)\\le C_{ij}, (i,j)\\in\\mathcal{L} (1h); \\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal{N}_i}p_n=\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{L}}q_{ij}, i\\in\\mathcal{N} (1i)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"RICITY MARKETS 2555 as violated. It can be noted that in this case h depends on the real s, power in","equation_id":"Eq. (3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Network constraints (1h)–(1i) can be condensed in a regularization function h, equal to 0 if they are respected and +∞ if they are violated. It can be noted that in this case h depends on the real power injections p_{n\\in\\Omega}. Then, problem (1) can be written as \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}f_n(p_n)+h(p_{n\\in\\Omega}) (3a); s.t. P=-P^{T} (3b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (3c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (3d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (3e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (3f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (3g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x-pnm","symbol":"p_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral real-power trade quantity of consumer n with partner m; for a consumer, selling quantities are constrained nonpositive by the consumer participation rule.","kind":"operational","domain":"n ∈ Ω_c and m ∈ ω_n; bilateral trade power","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x-pnm"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-theta-omega-c","symbol":"\\Omega_c","meaning":"Set of consumers, a subset of the P2P market community Ω; membership scopes the nonpositive trade restriction.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"subset of Ω"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::theta::v141-c-R006-theta-omega-c"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-theta-omega-n","symbol":"\\omega_n","meaning":"Set of trading partners of consumer n; it supplies the partner index set for the bilateral quantities p_{nm} restricted by the consumer rule.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of partners of consumer n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::theta::v141-c-R006-theta-omega-n"}]},{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"26-battery_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Consumers within the community may participate in local trading of locally produced energy. Scope: Consumer participation within the Flexi User community with individually owned batteries.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participation within the Flexi User community with individually owned batteries.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g02-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"a5a91514db4538f931a28439d7ab94506bb2fa385549ef837d0795af5d2c5885"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers within the community may participate in local trading of locally produced energy. Scope: Consumer participation within the Flexi User community with individually owned batteries.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g02-e01"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Market design for peer-to-peer trading","anchor_text":"he ability to sell excess electricity from renewable energy sources to peers. Each of the two setups","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Market design for peer-to-peer trading","anchor_text":"he ability to sell excess electricity from renewable energy sources to peers. Each of the two setups","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Decentralised storage – Flexi User Market: This market design applies rules to the system setup with individually owned batteries. Prosumers and consumers within a community can trade locally produced energy at a dynamic local P2P price. Privately owned storage can be charged by DG from prosumers within the community."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Consumers are permitted to participate in P2P trade within the community. Scope: Local P2P trade within the community. Condition: P2P trade is enabled, as in the P2P and STEP market cases.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Local P2P trade within the community.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e38"]},"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"80074edc3aba46c49066f66566ebbf6c2095b194b7b109afa34d8a0fde6a231e"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers are permitted to participate in P2P trade within the community. Scope: Local P2P trade within the community. Condition: P2P trade is enabled, as in the P2P and STEP market cases.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e38"],"ref":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"market. In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among ec- prosumers and consumers in a com","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, the market design allows P2P trade among prosumers and consumers in a community. Peers can trade locally produced and stored energy at a dynamic, half-hourly varying P2P price. Some prosumers and consumers have own batteries that can be charged with local generation and from the grid (see, e.g., [26])."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e38","location":{"location_id":"src-3c39fd6f72-p09-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"As of today, electricity end-users are at the end of the supply era chain in a still predominant top","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Today’s UK market under dynamic pricing – assuming smart metering – serves in this paper as our base case. To identify the value of each of the proposed local market features – P2P trade and battery storage –, three further cases are compared to the base case: We add P2P trade and battery storage possibilities one by one to investigate their sole values to the system. In short, we apply our model to four cases that distinguish by their options of battery storage and P2P trade. The possibility of feeding from the community into the grid is enabled in all cases. We define the following: • The Base case is defined as today’s electricity market under dynamic real-time pricing. It is not possible to directly trade with other peers, and no electricity storage possibilities are deployed within this case. • The BAT market introduces the possibility of battery storage. Direct trade between the peers is disabled. • The P2P market includes the direct P2P trade connection in the community. The possibility of storage is, however, not implemented. • The STEP market offers both battery storage and P2P trade options within the community."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::participation.consumer","paper_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"Consumer-role prosumers are permitted to trade in the LEM within their assigned DOEs. Scope: Consumer-side participation by prosumers in the proposed DOE-based LEM. Condition: Trading remains within the assigned DOEs.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer-side participation by prosumers in the proposed DOE-based LEM.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-E28"]},"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"27fa91eb91cb01a2eecc7c4d1115b041af0b4ad9fadfe4468374d90a119e56c8"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumer-role prosumers are permitted to trade in the LEM within their assigned DOEs. Scope: Consumer-side participation by prosumers in the proposed DOE-based LEM. Condition: Trading remains within the assigned DOEs.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-E28"],"ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"Contributions and Organization","anchor_text":"p nd networks to secure network operations by managing rb prosumers’ energy imports/exports. by • A ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed DOE-based LEM framework enables secure network operation for consumers trading in the LEM using assigned DOEs. The presented DOE calculation algorithm is computationally efficient and can be implemented for near real-time market clearing."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p07-c2r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"Prosumers Bidding Strategy","anchor_text":"p y , j,t j,t j,t j,t y g g quantities of prosumer j with the peers and the upstream grid Vs- in eac","equation_id":"(29)-(30)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Prosumers are allowed to trade in the LEM within the assigned DOEs P̂_{j,t} confirming the network-constrained operation. The sum of the buying energy of prosumer j at time t with the peers and the upstream grid should be equal to or less than the DOE limits P̂_{j,t}(> 0), and the sum of the selling energy with the peers and the upstream grid should be equal to or greater than the DOE limits P̂_{j,t}(≤ 0) as in (29) and (30), respectively. P^{pb}_{j,t} + P^{gb}_{j,t} ≤ P̂_{j,t}, if P̂_{j,t} > 0; ∀j∈J,∀t∈T, (29) P^{ps}_{j,t} + P^{gs}_{j,t} ≥ P̂_{j,t}, if P̂_{j,t} ≤ 0; ∀j∈J,∀t∈T. (30)"}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","paper_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","source_occurrences":[{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::V::participation.consumer","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Continued consumer participation is conditional on the local P2P transaction price not exceeding TLOU. Scope: Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage. Condition: The P2P transaction price must not exceed TLOU; buyers quit otherwise. Energy buyers may trade freely with energy sellers and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. Scope: Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market. | Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"994b36819e26ccabbe86c561facc4cb1f0a665134ef08996156d526cc28f0433"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Continued consumer participation is conditional on the local P2P transaction price not exceeding TLOU. Scope: Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage. Condition: The P2P transaction price must not exceed TLOU; buyers quit otherwise.","expression":"\\rho^{t}_{sb} \\le \\mathrm{TLOU}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-x-rho-sb-t"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-theta-TLOU"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e04"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Energy buyers may trade freely with energy sellers and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. Scope: Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2 System configuration","anchor_text":"b­ nt The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 in­ troduces a representative sy","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The most representative structure of the peer-to-peer energy trading market with shared energy storage units is shown in Fig. 1. In such a P2P market, a participant who has excessive energy and sells energy to other participants or the power grid is defined as a typical energy seller, e.g., a rooftop PV plant. A participant who consumes energy and buys energy from energy sellers or power grid is identified as a typical energy buyer, e.g., a community. Energy sellers and buyers in the P2P market can trade with each other freely and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. The solid arrows present the P2P transaction between typical energy sellers and energy buyers. All participants are connected with the local distribution network and the energy trading can be finished through physical power transmission lines."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x-rho-sb-t","symbol":"\\rho^{t}_{sb}","meaning":"P2P transaction price between seller s and buyer b in period t; the source calls it a decision variable and also states it is endogenous and not controlled by a single participant. The supplied source does not state a numerical domain beyond its role as a price.","kind":"operational","domain":"P2P transaction price between seller s and buyer b in period t; numerical domain not explicitly specified in the supplied evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["s","b","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x-rho-sb-t"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-theta-TLOU","symbol":"TLOU","meaning":"Time-and-level-of-use price ceiling in the buyer-participation condition: the source states that the P2P transaction price must be no more than TLOU, otherwise buyers will quit the P2P market. Its numeric value, unit and indexing are not specified in the supplied bound quote.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e04","v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g01-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::theta::v141-c-R006-theta-TLOU"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"consumer_role_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Consumers with non-flexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads, and/or renewable energy may participate in forward and real-time energy contract markets.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: consumer role","Core relationship: describes eligible participants by a combination of loads and/or energy resources","Eligibility predicate: the stated consumer may participate in energy contract transactions"],"variation_fields":["Resource scope includes non-flexible loads, time-coupled flexible loads, and/or renewable energy","Participation scope includes forward and real-time markets"],"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001"],"paper_id":"03-bilateral_p2p","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"deficit_buyer_participation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Interconnected microgrids with a power supply gap may act as energy buyers and participate in electric energy transactions with microgrids that have surplus renewable generation.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: potential energy-purchasing participant","Eligibility predicate: has a power supply gap","Participation role: purchases energy from a surplus-energy supplier"],"variation_fields":["The participating entity is an interconnected microgrid","Transactions occur within the distribution network","The potential supplier has surplus renewable generation"],"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"paper_id":"07-nash_bargaining","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"consumer_participation_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Local energy consumers may participate individually or as a local energy customer group and obtain peer status.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: local energy consumer","Eligibility relationship: may participate in transactions as a peer","Output: the consumer's peer participation identity"],"variation_fields":["The organizational form of participation is individual or customer group"],"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001"],"paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"consumer_participation_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Consumers may participate in transactions through Elecbay; permitted counterparties include suppliers, other consumers, and prosumers.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: energy consumer","Eligibility relationship: permitted to participate in platform energy transactions","Participation permission: may transact with suppliers and other peers"],"variation_fields":["The platform is Elecbay.","Applies to low-voltage distribution networks."],"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R011::v141-c-R011-C001"],"paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"flexible_demand_player_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Consumers with flexible demand participate as independent players in a non-cooperative bidding game and do not aggregate with other consumers or prosumers to obtain cooperative benefits.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: consumer","Eligibility predicate: has flexible demand","Participation relationship: included in the game player set as an independent player"],"variation_fields":["Adopts a non-cooperative, non-aggregating participation assumption","The set of all players is denoted N={1,2,…,i,…,n}, where n is the number of players","N is not composed solely of consumers"],"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C003"],"paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"flexible_demand_player_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Prosumers with flexible demand are included in the player set N and participate independently, without aggregating with consumers or other prosumers to obtain cooperative benefits.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: prosumer","Eligibility predicate: has flexible demand","Participation relationship: included in N as an independent player"],"variation_fields":["The simulation is a non-cooperative game for a single energy exchange period","N={1,2,…,i,…,n} denotes all players, not only prosumers","The case flexible demand is an electric water heater with a water tank, and scheduling keeps user demand satisfied","The case did not study energy storage systems or DG disconnection or derating scheduling"],"constraint_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C001","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C002","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017::v141-c-R017-C003"],"paper_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","function":"participation.prosumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"fixed_consumer_role","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The base model fixes the negotiating entity as either a producer or a consumer; entities belonging to Ω_c participate only in the purchase and consumption role, and their bilateral decisions satisfy P_nm≤0.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: negotiating entity designated as a consumer","Core relationship: restricts the entity to a single consumption role","Role predicate: n∈Ω_c, participates only in the capacity of a consumer"],"variation_fields":["The single-role assumption of producer or consumer in the base model","P_nm≤0 under the injection sign convention","The two-bus case includes residential and industrial consumers"],"constraint_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","13-consensus_product_differentiation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002"],"paper_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation","mechanism_id":"M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"purchase_only_participant_role","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The consumer class Ω_c is the purchase-only role in community Ω; for n∈Ω_c, its transactions with each partner must not be electricity sales, i.e., p_nm≤0.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: consumer role in the community","Core relationship: consumer membership limits buying and selling permissions","Role predicate: when n∈Ω_c, buying is permitted and selling is prohibited"],"variation_fields":["The source text describes consumers by non-positive net transaction power","The permission applies to bilateral transaction quantities for all m∈ω_n","Applicable to P2P scheduling in deterministic single-market time units"],"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"paper_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"community_consumer_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Consumers within the community may participate in community transactions involving locally produced electric energy.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: community consumers","Eligibility relationship: consumers within the community may participate in local electric energy transactions","Output: consumer participation eligibility"],"variation_fields":["Flexi User community scenario","The system is configured with individually owned batteries, but it is not explicitly stated that battery ownership is a consumer admission condition"],"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"paper_id":"26-battery_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"conditional_community_peer_participation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Community consumers have intra-community P2P trading participation eligibility when P2P trading is enabled; the P2P and STEP cases satisfy this enabling condition.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: community consumers","Core relationship: permitted to participate in intra-community P2P trading","Eligibility predicate: is a community consumer and P2P trading is enabled"],"variation_fields":["The enabling cases are P2P and STEP","Participation scope is limited to within the community"],"constraint_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"paper_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"operating_envelope_participation_eligibility","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Prosumers in the consumer role may participate in the LEM, provided that transactions remain within their allocated DOE.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: market participant in the consumer role","Core relationship: participation permission under operating envelope constraints","Eligibility condition: transactions comply with the allocated DOE"],"variation_fields":["The applicable role is the consumer among prosumers","DOE is used to support local transactions under network constraints"],"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"],"paper_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","stage":"L1","subtype":"buyer_role_and_price_conditioned_participation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The buyer is a participant that consumes energy and purchases electricity from sellers or the grid, and may freely participate in bilateral transactions; continued participation in the P2P market requires \\rho^t_{sb}\\le\\mathrm{TLOU}; otherwise, exit.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: buyer role that consumes energy and purchases electricity","Core relationship: buyer transaction permission and conditions for continued participation","Necessary participation predicate: the P2P transaction price does not exceed the grid TLOU price"],"variation_fields":["The community is an example of a buyer","Participants connect to the local distribution network","Prices are indexed by seller, buyer, and time period","No numerical value is given for the TLOU threshold"],"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002"],"paper_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"participation.consumer","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["Resources include non-flexible load, time-coupled flexible load, and/or renewable energy; it is not required to possess all resources simultaneously.","Participation scope includes forward and real-time energy contract markets; two-way trading authority is not inferred from renewable energy configuration."],"evidence_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["An interconnected microgrid with a supply gap may trade with a microgrid that has surplus renewable generation.","The transaction is located within the distribution network, and this unit focuses on the deficit energy buyer."],"evidence_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["A local energy consumer may participate individually or as a local energy customer group.","Group participation does not automatically mean participation through a dedicated agent representing other users."],"evidence_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["Consumers may trade through Elecbay, with counterparties including suppliers, other consumers, and prosumers.","Applicable to low-voltage distribution networks; the consumer is not inferred to have electricity-sale authority from the counterparty category."],"evidence_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["The consumer must have flexible demand, participate independently in non-cooperative bidding, and not aggregate with other consumers or prosumers to obtain cooperative benefits.","N={1,2,…,i,…,n} is the set of all players, and n is the number of players; it does not contain only consumers."],"evidence_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer","retained_variants":["Prosumers with flexible demand are included in N and participate independently, without aggregating with consumers or other prosumers to obtain cooperative benefits.","Simulation uses a non-cooperative game for a single energy exchange period; N={1,2,…,i,…,n} denotes all players.","Case flexible demand is an electric water heater with a water tank, and scheduling keeps user demand satisfied; the case did not study energy storage systems or DG disconnection or derating scheduling, and these are not treated as general admission exclusions."],"evidence_refs":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["The base model fixes the negotiating entity as either a producer or a consumer; when n∈Ω_c, it participates only as a consumer.","Under the injection sign convention, the bilateral decision satisfies P_nm≤0.","The two-bus case includes residential and industrial consumers; the fixed single-role assumption applies only to this base model."],"evidence_refs":["13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["Ω_c is the consumer class in community Ω; when n∈Ω_c, p_nm≤0 holds for all m∈ω_n.","The source represents purchase-only permission by non-positive net transaction power, applicable to deterministic single-market time-unit P2P scheduling."],"evidence_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["Applicable to the Flexi User community, where the traded object is locally produced electric energy.","The system uses individually owned batteries, but it is not explicitly stated that battery ownership is a consumer admission condition."],"evidence_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["Community consumers obtain intra-community P2P participation eligibility only when P2P trading is enabled.","The P2P and STEP cases satisfy the enabling condition, and the participation scope is limited to within the community; this unit does not grant collective wholesale access rights."],"evidence_refs":["32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["Applicable to the consumer role among prosumers; transactions must remain within the allocated DOE.","DOE supports local transactions under network constraints and does not add electricity-selling permission based on the prosumer label."],"evidence_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::participation.consumer::projection"]},{"unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","retained_variants":["The buyer consumes energy and purchases electricity from sellers or the grid, and may freely participate in bilateral transactions; the community is an example of a buyer, and participants connect to the local distribution network.","Continued participation requires \\rho^t_{sb}\\le\\mathrm{TLOU}; otherwise, exit; prices are indexed by seller, buyer, and time period.","TLOU is the grid price threshold; no numerical value is given."],"evidence_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer::projection"]}],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false,"baseline_parent_id":"COMP-a774da994c2ca212","baseline_parent_code":"PAR034","secondary_class":"SC-L1-2a489524c6392357","category":{"id":"SC-L1-2a489524c6392357","stage":"L1","label_zh":"Electricity-demand-side eligibility","definition_zh":"It confirms that the subject assumes an electric-energy demand-side participation identity through energy consumption, supplementing a deficit, or flexible demand, and its trading authority, resource conditions, and participation form are retained by source.","main_identity_axes":["Economic object: electric energy used for consumption or for making up a deficit","Economic role: participant in consumption, energy purchasing, or flexible demand","Participation purpose: obtaining or delimiting the demand-side participation identity"],"inclusion_criteria":["The main content is explicitly the participation eligibility of subjects of consumption, deficit energy purchasing, or flexible energy use.","It may include purchase-only authority, participation by an individual or a customer group, and independent-player conditions.","Subjects that have generation assets may also be included, but the unit must explicitly focus on the demand-side role."],"exclusion_criteria":["The main content is a generation or surplus-electricity supply identity, or an explicit configuration of dual supply and demand roles.","Consumers participate in the capacity of regulation-service suppliers, and their traded object is flexibility or ancillary services.","A demand-side role is inferred solely from the subject's name, or only a specialized agency relationship of representing others is specified."],"supporting_unit_ids":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer","13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer","06-sdr_energy_sharing::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer"],"status":"agent_defined_structurally_validated"},"definition":{"definition_zh":"Confirm that the entity participates in electricity trading as a consumer, a deficit energy buyer, or a flexible-demand player; organizational form, purchase-only authority, market activation, and conditions for continued participation are limited according to the source.","identity_fields":["Economic object: electricity used for consumption or to satisfy flexible demand","Economic role: consumer, deficit buyer, or flexible-demand player","Main permission: demand-side trading participation and source-limited authority"],"member_unit_ids":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::participation.consumer","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.consumer","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M03::participation.prosumer","13-consensus_product_differentiation::M01::participation.consumer","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::participation.consumer","26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","32-wholesale_prosumer_storage::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::participation.consumer","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::participation.consumer"]}},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/201"});
